														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2339896">
									

<p>I have been wavering about this one. I like the basic idea (although it's similar to a few other items) and the proposed containment revisions section is a neat illustration, if a bit familiar. I'm also not sure about the Serpent's Hand having a &quot;field team&quot; - I thought they were a bit more amorphous than that. But I'm starting to have concerns about how this skip actually works:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Additionally, SCP-2414 functions as an infohazard that affects Foundation personnel who become aware of its existence as an object considered by the Foundation to be anomalous &#8230; personnel unable to [liberate it] will attempt to recruit assistance from those who are.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think I get this. Senior personnel who were told &quot;the trapezohedron is not anomalous&quot; would not be subject to the infohazard. It's only when they're told &quot;&#8230; but the Foundation thinks it is&quot; that the problems start.</p>
<p>Doesn't that lead to a tipping point - if enough senior personnel are affected, then &quot;the Foundation&quot; no longer thinks that it's anomalous, so the infohazard won't spread any further. Of course, by that stage the mind-wipe effect could be problematic, depending on how this next bit is intended:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the event that SCP-2414 is successfully removed from Foundation custody, all memory and knowledge of the Foundation will be erased from the individual or individuals responsible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Only the individuals responsible for it being removed from Foundation custody? That would seem to be a relatively small proportion of the number of people infected by the infohazard - it would only take a small subset (albeit including some very senior personnel) to actually cause the release. If the intention was to mindwipe all personnel with any knowledge of SCP-2414, that would be different, but that's not how you've phrased it.</p>
<p>It strikes me that if &quot;considered by the Foundation to be anomalous&quot; is the primer for the infohazard, and release is the trigger for the mindwipe, there are two slightly better ways of containment. One is to make this an -EX, so that the Foundation does not consider it anomalous. The second is to simply take the hit on mindwipes and get it out of Foundation custody in a way that makes it unlikely to come back - space or deep ocean come to mind.</p>

							</div>

								
					
